{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-4975a108-8fb7-42b9-8332-0ef2a7a82f3b","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-a0fc65064932839c","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-8010-yl27p7nu/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8010-decomposition.md","content":"# Decomposition Summary — RHAISTRAT-8010\n\nOperator reconciliation log standardization with structured resource context.\n\n## Triage Outcome\n\nNot below-threshold and not docs-only. Although the strategy is S-sized, single-component\n(rhods-operator), and single active team (ODH Operator team), the scope spans multiple\narchitecturally distinct work streams: mechanical code conversion across two binaries\n(manager, cloudmanager) plus substantive documentation authoring (a new contribution\nguideline documenting the convention, published for external operator adoption per HLR-8).\nPer Step 0's second escape condition, full decomposition applies.\n\n## Component Mapping\n\n- Strategy component `rhods-operator` (ODH Operator team) is **active** (code changes) and\n  maps to canonical Jira component **`OpenShift AI Productization`** — the canonical\n  component list (`.context/rhai-components.txt`) has no verbatim `rhods-operator` entry,\n  and `OpenShift AI Productization` is the closest canonical name for the team that owns\n  the rhods-operator repository (the RHOAI platform operator productized from\n  opendatahub-io/opendatahub-operator). All three epics use this canonical value.\n- Impacted team **Cluster Observability Operator team** (Loki Operator, Tempo Operator) is\n  **passive**: per platform policy overlay 0008 and the RFE approval comment, RHOAI\n  publishes the convention for voluntary adoption and does not install, manage, or control\n  external operator dependencies. No COO-team epic is generated; publication is a\n  deliverable of the documentation epic, and adoption is at COO's discretion.\n- rhods-operator exists in architecture context\n  (`.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.6-ea.1/rhods-operator.md`) — no\n  onboarding chain (repo/license/konflux) is needed.\n\n## Staff Engineer Input Disposition\n\nThe Staff Engineer / SME Input section is non-empty and takes precedence. Overrides and\nconfirmations applied:\n\n1. **Action file count corrected**: exactly 15 action files across four tiers (8 component\n   `components/<name>/<name>_controller_actions.go`, 3 service\n   `services/{auth,gateway,monitoring}/*_controller_actions.go`, 1 DSC\n   `datasciencecluster/datasciencecluster_controller_actions.go`, 3 module\n   `modules/modules_controller_actions*.go`) — not \"~16 component action files\". The\n   decomposition uses the corrected counts.\n2. **Module controller tier added to scope**: the module controller with its 8 module\n   handlers (Dashboard, AIGateway, KServe, OGX, Workbenches, MLflowOperator, FeastOperator,\n   MCPLifecycleOperator) is a fifth controller tier with its own error logs and is covered\n   by HLR-6 in the manager-binary epic.\n3. **Resolved non-unknown**: the question \"how many component action files exist?\" is\n   answered (8/8 components have exactly one action file, confirmed by glob) and is NOT\n   treated as an open question — no Investigation epic for it.\n4. **Confirmed as-is**: three-problem diagnosis (message-embedded identity, DSCI\n   non-standard keys `Request.Name`/`DSCInitialization`, missing `resourceKind`), camelCase\n   field naming, cloudmanager's shared reconciler already providing `name`/`namespace`,\n   cluster-scoped-resource acceptance criterion, and the S effort estimate.\n\nNote: the architecture-context file's analysis section describes \"three controller tiers\"\nwhile the SME input and strategy specify five (module controller and DSCI counted\nseparately); the SME tier count takes precedence. The context file independently\ncorroborates the DSCInitialization CRD, the component and service controllers, logr usage,\nand the cloudmanager binary, so the decomposition premise stands.\n\n## Epic List\n\n| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n| RHAISTRAT-8010-E001 | Standardize manager binary reconciliation error logs with structured resource context | Implementation | ODH Operator team | P0 |\n| RHAISTRAT-8010-E002 | Add structured resource context to cloudmanager controller reconciliation error logs | Implementation | ODH Operator team | P0 |\n| RHAISTRAT-8010-E003 | Document and publish the structured reconciliation log field convention | Implementation (docs-authoring) | ODH Operator team | P1 |\n\n## Dependency DAG\n\n```mermaid\ngraph TD\n    E001[\"E001 manager binary log standardization\"] --> E003[\"E003 convention documentation and publication\"]\n    E002[\"E002 cloudmanager log standardization\"] --> E003\n```\n\n## DAG Justification\n\n| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n|------|------|-----------|\n| E001 → E003 | Rule 11 (docs last) | The docs-authoring epic is blocked by every Implementation epic: the contribution guideline documents the convention as implemented, including before/after code examples from the real conversion and the DSCI key migration guide. |\n| E002 → E003 | Rule 11 (docs last) | Same — the guideline also covers the cloudmanager controllers' use of the shared reconciler. |\n| (no edge) E001 ∥ E002 | Rule 15 (same repo, different areas) | Manager and cloudmanager are separate binaries in the same repository with disjoint controller sets; they run in parallel. Merge conflicts on shared files are a coordination risk, not a dependency. |\n\nNo Investigation epics (see Open Question Disposition below). No konflux/repo/license\nonboarding epics — the component is in the platform with an existing build pipeline, so\nRules 5-10 ordering and the Rule 25 build-pipeline AC do not apply.\n\n## HLR Traceability Matrix\n\n| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) | Notes |\n|-----|----------|---------|-------|\n| HLR-1 — reconciliation error logs embedding name/namespace in message strings converted to structured KV pairs | P0 | E001, E002 | Manager binary sites in E001; cloudmanager controller sites in E002. |\n| HLR-2 — DSCI migrated from `Request.Name`/`DSCInitialization` to camelCase `name`/`namespace` | P0 | E001 | |\n| HLR-3 — service controller (Gateway, Auth, Monitoring, CertConfigMapGenerator, Setup) error logs include `resourceKind` | P0 | E001 | |\n| HLR-4 — all 15 action file error logs include `name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind` across 4 tiers | P0 | E001 | 8 component + 3 service + 1 DSC + 3 module action files, all in the manager binary. |\n| HLR-5 — cloudmanager controller (AWSKubernetesEngine, AzureKubernetesEngine, CoreWeaveKubernetesEngine) error logs include `name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind` | P0 | E002 | |\n| HLR-6 — module controller error logs include `name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind` (8 module handlers) | P0 | E001 | |\n| HLR-7 — DSC and component controller error logs include `resourceKind` | P1 | E001 | **bundled**: adds `resourceKind` to the exact same `log.Error` call sites, same audit pass, and same CI lint rule as the P0 conversion work in E001 — the DSC and 8 component controllers are part of the single mechanical pass over the manager binary's reconciliation error paths, so splitting would double-touch every call site and duplicate the audit; not separately schedulable. |\n| HLR-8 — convention documented as contribution guideline and published for external operator adoption | P1 | E003 | docs-authoring epic; priority derived via the docs-authoring exception (strategy priority Major → P1). |\n\nAll 8 HLRs are covered; no orphaned requirements.\n\n## Open Question Disposition\n\nAll four strategy open questions were evaluated against the decision rule \"does the answer\nchange which downstream epics exist or what they do?\" None qualifies for an Investigation\nepic:\n\n1. **Fields beyond name/namespace/kind (attempt count, error category)** — PM/Observability\n   product decision. The P0/P1 field set is fixed by the HLRs, so the mandated conversion\n   proceeds identically either way; the decision only affects the guideline's content.\n   Disposition: open question with PM owner recorded on the documentation epic (E003),\n   which must resolve and document it (include or explicitly defer).\n2. **Which existing log-based alerts rely on message-string patterns** — resolved by the\n   log-consumer regression validation that is already part of the implementation work\n   (identify with SRE/Support before merge). The strategy prescribes the approach\n   regardless of the answer. Disposition: acceptance criterion on E001 and E002.\n3. **Cloudmanager same release or subsequent** — PM scheduling decision. It does not change\n   what is built; splitting cloudmanager into its own epic makes either answer a planning\n   choice. Disposition: noted in E002 Risks/Notes.\n4. **Actual percentage of embedded-identity log calls** — the HLRs require converting ALL\n   such sites regardless of percentage; the audit is the first phase of the implementation\n   work. Disposition: in-scope audit step and acceptance criterion on E001/E002 (grep/static\n   analysis finds zero remaining embedding sites).\n\n## Health Warnings\n\n- **Epic size overage kept deliberately (E001)**: the manager-binary epic sits above the\n  ~2-week guideline (strategy estimate: ~0.5 sprint audit + ~0.75-1 sprint conversion\n  across five controller tiers and 15 action files + CI lint rule). It is kept as one epic\n  because it is a single logical change — one mechanical pass over one surface (reconciliation\n  error log calls in the manager binary), where a split would duplicate the call-site audit\n  and fragment the CI lint rule across epics. **Re-split trigger**: re-split E001 along\n  controller-tier lines (e.g., component+DSC controllers / module controller+handlers /\n  service controllers+DSCI) if the call-site audit finds materially more `log.Error` sites\n  than the 75%-of-calls estimate implies (e.g., more than about twice the estimated site\n  count) or if conversion cannot complete within the sprint.\n- **DSCI key-name migration is a breaking replacement (Rule 23)**: existing Loki/CloudWatch\n  queries filtering on `Request.Name`/`DSCInitialization` will break. Mitigation is\n  recorded on E001: transition in a single release, migration documented in release notes\n  with before/after query examples (expanded in the documentation epic). A feature flag is\n  not applicable to log field names; the single-release cutover is the compat mechanism.\n- **External adoption cannot be gated**: COO team adoption of the convention is voluntary\n  per platform policy overlay 0008. The documentation epic publishes the convention and\n  files tracking issues; adoption timing is outside this strategy's control. No tiered\n  delivery split is needed because no RHOAI work is gated by COO — the strategy's full\n  value (HLR-1..7) is delivered by E001/E002 regardless.\n- **Docs sequencing tension**: a risk mitigation suggests publishing the convention early\n  to COO. Per Rule 11 the docs-authoring epic is sequenced after both implementation epics\n  because the guideline's before/after examples and migration guide are drawn from the\n  implemented conversion. If early external publication becomes urgent, the team can send\n  an interim convention note to COO without restructuring this DAG.\n\n## Tiered Delivery\n\nNot applicable. The only external dependency with uncertain timing (COO team adoption) is\nvoluntary and outside RHOAI's control per platform policy; no RHOAI epic is gated by it, so\nthere is no Tier 1/Tier 2 split. The controller-runtime logr dependency is internal and\nalready exists.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":42710,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b715a6c9be533eb8","