{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-8c9f4a13-32e1-41db-b8bb-a09c892ef04c","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-a3a44c3985d271cb","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-8010-yl27p7nu/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8010-E002.md","content":"# Add structured resource context to cloudmanager controller reconciliation error logs\n\n## Description\n\nThis epic applies the structured resource-context logging convention to the rhods-operator\ncloudmanager binary — the companion binary handling multi-cloud infrastructure dependencies\non non-OpenShift Kubernetes (AWS EKS, Azure AKS, CoreWeave). Its reconciliation controllers\n(AWSKubernetesEngine, AzureKubernetesEngine, CoreWeaveKubernetesEngine) are built on the\nsame controller-runtime/logr foundation using the shared reconciler pattern, so they already\nreceive `name` and `namespace` from controller-runtime's request logger. This epic converts\nany error-path log calls that embed resource identity in the message string to structured\nkey-value pairs and adds the `resourceKind` field to all reconciliation error logs.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Audit all `log.Error()` call sites in reconciliation error paths across the three\n  cloudmanager controllers (AWSKubernetesEngine, AzureKubernetesEngine,\n  CoreWeaveKubernetesEngine).\n- Convert call sites embedding resource name or namespace in the message string to\n  structured key-value pairs (`name`, `namespace`), removing the redundant identity from\n  the message text.\n- Add `resourceKind` as a structured field to all reconciliation error logs in these\n  controllers (kind is not reliably implied by controller name for cloud infrastructure\n  resources).\n- Verify that `name`/`namespace` from the shared reconciler's request logger are present on\n  error paths, and fill any gaps found by the audit.\n- Add unit tests asserting field presence and correctness via test logger output.\n- Verify that cloud-environment log consumers (e.g., CloudWatch on EKS/AKS deployments)\n  ingest the changed log format without configuration changes.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- Static analysis (grep/lint) over the cloudmanager binary finds zero `log.Error`\n  reconciliation-error call sites that embed resource name or namespace in the message\n  string; every such site emits `name` and `namespace` as structured key-value pairs.\n- Unit tests parsing test logger output assert `name`, `namespace`, and `resourceKind` are\n  present with correct values in error logs from all three cloudmanager controllers\n  (AWSKubernetesEngine, AzureKubernetesEngine, CoreWeaveKubernetesEngine).\n- The cloud-environment log consumer verification is documented, confirming ingestion of\n  the changed log format without configuration changes for a sample reconciliation error.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- HLR-1 [P0]: reconciliation error logs embedding name/namespace in message strings\n  converted to structured key-value pairs (cloudmanager binary portion).\n- HLR-5 [P0]: cloudmanager controller error logs include `name`, `namespace`, and\n  `resourceKind` as structured fields.\n\n## Risks / Notes\n\n- Release sequencing (same release as the manager binary work or a subsequent release) is\n  an open product decision owned by PM / the ODH Operator team; this epic is independently\n  schedulable so either answer is a planning choice that does not change the work. Impact\n  scope is narrower than the manager binary because cloudmanager is only deployed on\n  non-OpenShift Kubernetes.\n- The shared reconciler already provides `name`/`namespace`, so the incremental change is\n  expected to be small (~0.25 sprint per the strategy estimate).\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":51433,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b715a6c9be533eb8","agent_id":"a7fb5fe8d6