triage: null` (full decomposition) with `epic_count: 3` — no contradiction; multi-epic\n38\toutput is expected. The single failure below is a rule-mandated acceptance criterion the\n39\tvalidator cannot check.\n40\t\n41\t## Criterion Details\n42\t\n43\t### 1. HLR Coverage (2/2)\n44\t\n45\tAll 8 HLRs map to epics: HLR-1 → E001+E002 (split by binary, both portions traceable),\n46\tHLR-2/3/4/6 → E001, HLR-5 → E002, HLR-7 → E001 (bundled), HLR-8 → E003. Traceability\n47\tmatrix present with per-epic HLR Traceability sections matching it. Priority inheritance\n48\tis clean: E001/E002 are P0 with no dependencies; E003 (P1, docs-authoring) derives its\n49\tpriority from the strategy's Major priority per the docs-authoring exception, and its\n50\tRule 11 edges do not trigger priority inheritance. The HLR-7 (P1) bundling into E001 is\n51\tjustified, not priority collapse: it edits the exact same `log.Error` call sites, shares\n52\tthe same audit pass and CI lint rule as the P0 conversion, and deferring it would mean\n53\trevisiting every converted call site — incidental polish on the P0 edit, not a distinct\n54\tdeferrable feature. The justification is recorded in the matrix.\n55\t\n56\t### 2. DAG Coherence (2/2)\n57\t\n58\tNo cycles. Both edges (E001→E003, E002→E003) are Rule 11 docs-last edges and are\n59\tjustified in the DAG Justification table. Frontmatter `dependencies` match the diagram\n60\texactly (E003 lists both E001 and E002; E001/E002 empty), and the diagram uses the\n61\tcorrect dependency→dependent arrow convention. E001 ∥ E002 parallel is correct per Rule\n62\t15 (same repo, disjoint binaries/controller sets); no konflux/repo/license epics exist,\n63\tso Rules 5-10/25 are inapplicable. Critical path length 2 fits the S-size heuristic\n64\t(1-2). No branch files exist and none are needed (no bounded-outcome Investigations).\n65\tRule 11 edges correctly present and not flagged as serialization. Cross-check of epic\n66\tcontent found no build-against artifact dependency between E001 and E002 (the CI lint\n67\trule coupling is a scope/ownership ambiguity, flagged under Completeness, not a\n68\tdata/artifact dependency warranting an edge — an edge would wrongly serialize\n69\trule-mandated parallel work).\n70\t\n71\t### 3. Epic Boundaries (2/2)\n72\t\n73\tAll three epics share team (ODH Operator team) and canonical component\n74\t(`OpenShift AI Productization`, verified present in `.context/rhai-components.txt`);\n75\tthe split is along binary/work-product lines (manager binary / cloudmanager binary /\n76\tdocs), which is the correct axis. No scope duplication: E001 and E002 touch disjoint\n77\tbinaries and controller sets; E003 is pure documentation. No work-product type mixing:\n78\tcode and content authoring are in separate epics. E001 exceeds the ~2-week guideline but\n79\tis a single logical change (one mechanical pass over one surface), and the Health\n80\tWarning records a concrete, measurable re-split trigger (re-split along controller-tier\n81\tlines if the audit finds materially more sites than the 75% estimate implies, or if\n82\tconversion cannot complete within the sprint) — the managed-tradeoff carve-out applies,\n83\tso the criterion is not docked.\n84\t\n85\t### 4. Type Correctness (2/2)\n86\t\n87\tAll epics are Implementation; E003 correctly carries `implementation_type:\n88\tdocs-authoring`. No Investigation epics, and the open-question disposition correctly\n89\tapplies the \"does the answer change downstream structure?\" test: the percentage question\n90\tis answered by the audit itself, the alert-rule question by the validation work, the\n91\tfield-set and release-sequencing questions are PM decisions recorded on E003/E002.\n92\t`gated_by` is null everywhere and no epic lists an Investigation dependency, so no\n93\tgate metadata is required. All Implementation epics produce concrete artifacts.\n94\t\n95\t### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (2/2)\n96\t\n97\tType-appropriate signal fields throughout (all Implementation epics carry `ai_signals`;\n98\t`investigation_signals` null). Signal values check against the 9-signal rubric and the\n99\tstrategy content: E001's `open_questions: 1` is right (remaining unknowns are resolved by\n100\tthe epic's own audit, not approach-changing), `external_dependency: 0` is defensible\n101\tgiven the SRE/Support coordination (E002's `1` is consistent — it has no such\n102\tcoordination), `architecture_claims: 1` on all three epics cites\n103\t`.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.6-ea.1/rhods-operator.md`, which\n104\texists and corroborates the logr usage, DSCInitialization CRD/controller, service\n105\tcontrollers, and the cloudmanager binary — calibration check passes. E003's\n106\t`open_questions: -1` and `human_process_gates: -1` are correct (the additional-fields\n107\tPM decision gates guideline content; technical review and PM sign-off are human steps).\n108\tRationales in all three ai-signals files match the frontmatter values.\n109\t\n110\t### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (0/2)\n111\t\n112\tACs are present and testable across all epics, and the Rule 24 docs-review AC is present\n113\ton E003 (\"technically reviewed against the implemented logging changes\"). However, the\n114\tRule 23 mandate is not met: the decomposition's own Health Warning labels the DSCI\n115\tkey-name migration \"a breaking replacement (Rule 23)\" — existing Loki/CloudWatch queries\n116\tfiltering on `Request.Name`/`DSCInitialization` will break — yet E001 carries no\n117\trollback/feature-flag (or equivalent compat-mechanism) acceptance criterion. The chosen\n118\tcompat mechanism (single-release cutover plus release-notes migration documentation with\n119\tbefore/after query examples) is recorded only in E001's Risks/Notes and the summary\n120\tHealth Warnings, never as a testable AC. Rule 23 requires the replacing Implementation\n121\tepic to carry this as an AC; a missing rule-mandated AC is a major issue and forces this\n122\tcriterion to 0. Correction: add an AC to E001 making the compat mechanism testable\n123\t(e.g., \"the DSCI key-name migration lands as a single-release cutover, with the\n124\tmigration and before/after query examples documented in the release notes\") or an\n125\texplicit rollback-plan AC. No other defects: no externally-controlled preconditions\n126\tmisfiled as ACs (the before/after log comparison and CloudWatch ingestion ACs are\n127\tconditions on what the epics themselves verify), and no sibling-epic draft-ID references\n128\tin epic bodies.\n129\t\n130\t### 7. Completeness (2/2)\n131\t\n132\tFull scope coverage: every strategy acceptance criterion (conversion, DSCI migration,\n133\tservice-controller resourceKind, 15 action files across 4 tiers, cloudmanager\n134\tcontrollers, cluster-scoped resources, contribution guideline) maps to an epic AC, with\n135\tthe SME-corrected action-file counts and the fifth (module controller) tier carried\n136\tforward. All four open questions are explicitly dispositioned rather than dropped;\n137\tstrategy risks (inconsistent adoption, log consumer regression, external adoption,\n138\tDSCI query breakage) are carried into epic Risks/Notes; the backwards-compatibility NFR\n139\tis operationalized as ACs on E001/E002. The COO team is correctly treated as passive\n140\t(per overlay 0008) with publication as an E003 deliverable. One minor observation\n141\t(not score-affecting): the CI lint rule's file scope is ambiguous across the parallel\n142\tepics — E001 creates it with an AC of \"passing on the converted codebase\" while E002's\n143\tfirst AC references \"grep/lint\" over the cloudmanager binary that neither epic's lint\n144\townership clearly covers; if repo-wide, E001's passing AC implicitly couples to E002's\n145\tparallel conversion. Clarify the rule's per-binary scope or record the coordination in\n146\tboth epics' Risks/Notes.\n147\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-affd2cb628b8f40f","agent_id":"af47b7cebcc45ac50","s