{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-88a5c4af26608447","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-8010-E002\n3\ttitle: Add structured resource context to cloudmanager controller reconciliation error\n4\t  logs\n5\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8010\n6\tcomponent: OpenShift AI Productization\n7\tteam: ODH Operator team\n8\ttype: Implementation\n9\tpriority: P0\n10\tdependencies: []\n11\tai_signals:\n12\t  change_specificity: 1\n13\t  pattern_precedent: 1\n14\t  adapter_pattern: 0\n15\t  existing_foundation: 1\n16\t  open_questions: 1\n17\t  external_dependency: 1\n18\t  human_process_gates: 0\n19\t  repo_access: 1\n20\t  architecture_claims: 1\n21\timplementation_type: null\n22\tinvestigation_signals: null\n23\tai_implementability: High\n24\tai_implementability_score: 7\n25\tjira_key: null\n26\tbranch: null\n27\tgated_by: null\n28\tgate_failure_impact: null\n29\t---\n30\t# Add structured resource context to cloudmanager controller reconciliation error logs\n31\t\n32\t## Description\n33\t\n34\tThis epic applies the structured resource-context logging convention to the rhods-operator\n35\tcloudmanager binary — the companion binary handling multi-cloud infrastructure dependencies\n36\ton non-OpenShift Kubernetes (AWS EKS, Azure AKS, CoreWeave). Its reconciliation controllers\n37\t(AWSKubernetesEngine, AzureKubernetesEngine, CoreWeaveKubernetesEngine) are built on the\n38\tsame controller-runtime/logr foundation using the shared reconciler pattern, so they already\n39\treceive `name` and `namespace` from controller-runtime's request logger. This epic converts\n40\tany error-path log calls that embed resource identity in the message string to structured\n41\tkey-value pairs and adds the `resourceKind` field to all reconciliation error logs.\n42\t\n43\t## Scope\n44\t\n45\t- Audit all `log.Error()` call sites in reconciliation error paths across the three\n46\t  cloudmanager controllers (AWSKubernetesEngine, AzureKubernetesEngine,\n47\t  CoreWeaveKubernetesEngine).\n48\t- Convert call sites embedding resource name or namespace in the message string to\n49\t  structured key-value pairs (`name`, `namespace`), removing the redundant identity from\n50\t  the message text.\n51\t- Add `resourceKind` as a structured field to all reconciliation error logs in these\n52\t  controllers (kind is not reliably implied by controller name for cloud infrastructure\n53\t  resources).\n54\t- Verify that `name`/`namespace` from the shared reconciler's request logger are present on\n55\t  error paths, and fill any gaps found by the audit.\n56\t- Add unit tests asserting field presence and correctness via test logger output.\n57\t- Verify that cloud-environment log consumers (e.g., CloudWatch on EKS/AKS deployments)\n58\t  ingest the changed log format without configuration changes.\n59\t\n60\t## Acceptance Criteria\n61\t\n62\t- Static analysis (grep/lint) over the cloudmanager binary finds zero `log.Error`\n63\t  reconciliation-error call sites that embed resource name or namespace in the message\n64\t  string; every such site emits `name` and `namespace` as structured key-value pairs.\n65\t- Unit tests parsing test logger output assert `name`, `namespace`, and `resourceKind` are\n66\t  present with correct values in error logs from all three cloudmanager controllers\n67\t  (AWSKubernetesEngine, AzureKubernetesEngine, CoreWeaveKubernetesEngine).\n68\t- The cloud-environment log consumer verification is documented, confirming ingestion of\n69\t  the changed log format without configuration changes for a sample reconciliation error.\n70\t\n71\t## HLR Traceability\n72\t\n73\t- HLR-1 [P0]: reconciliation error logs embedding name/namespace in message strings\n74\t  converted to structured key-value pairs (cloudmanager binary portion).\n75\t- HLR-5 [P0]: cloudmanager controller error logs include `name`, `namespace`, and\n76\t  `resourceKind` as structured fields.\n77\t\n78\t## Risks / Notes\n79\t\n80\t- Release sequencing (same release as the manager binary work or a subsequent release) is\n81\t  an open product decision owned by PM / the ODH Operator team; this epic is independently\n82\t  schedulable so either answer is a planning choice that does not change the work. Impact\n83\t  scope is narrower than the manager binary because cloudmanager is only deployed on\n84\t  non-OpenShift Kubernetes.\n85\t- The shared reconciler already provides `name`/`namespace`, so the incremental change is\n86\t  expected to be small (~0.25 sprint per the strategy estimate).\n87\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-affd2cb628b8f40f","agent_id":"af47b7cebcc45ac50","session_id":"97c222b8-155b-40d2-9c11-f4c45b15208c","uuid":"3c9ff463-06e1-45e6-87da-0d536e09ce0f","timestamp":"2026-10-25T19:37:28.192Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-88a5c4af26608447","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Revise RHAISTRAT-8010 decomposition"}
