{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-906ec05f1aa4f933","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# AI Signal Rationale — RHAISTRAT-8010-E001\n2\t\n3\tStandardize manager binary reconciliation error logs with structured resource context.\n4\t\n5\t| Signal | Value | Rationale |\n6\t|--------|-------|-----------|\n7\t| change_specificity | 1 | Exact controller names (DSC, 8 component, module + 8 handlers, DSCI, 5 service), exact action-file paths (e.g., `components/<name>/<name>_controller_actions.go`, `modules/modules_controller_actions*.go`), exact field names (`name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind`), and even the DSCI offending lines (`dscinitialization_controller.go:154,169,182`) are specified by the strategy and Staff Engineer input. |\n8\t| pattern_precedent | 1 | Framework controllers already emit `name`/`namespace` via controller-runtime's request logger, and `modules_controller_actions_inject_env.go` already contains `log.Error` calls with structured fields — the conversion follows an existing in-codebase pattern. |\n9\t| adapter_pattern | 0 | No adapter/plugin or reference-implementation integration is involved; this is a direct mechanical edit of log call sites. |\n10\t| existing_foundation | 1 | Extends the existing controller-runtime/logr logging already used throughout the manager binary's controller hierarchy; no new logging infrastructure. |\n11\t| open_questions | 1 | No unresolved question changes this epic's approach: the field set is fixed by the HLRs, and the remaining unknowns (actual percentage of embedding call sites, which alert rules rely on message patterns) are resolved by this epic's own audit and validation work — failure mode is fix-and-retry, not re-planning. |\n12\t| external_dependency | 0 | No upstream contribution or vendor coordination is needed for the code change; controller-runtime's logr interface already exists. SRE/Support coordination on alert rules is informational coordination, not a dependency that changes the build. |\n13\t| human_process_gates | 0 | No human approval step gates the code change itself; the SRE/Support alert-rule identification and dev-cluster log-consumer verification are engineering verification activities performed within the epic, not approval gates. |\n14\t| repo_access | 1 | The rhods-operator repository is the pipeline's working target with a public midstream mirror (opendatahub-io/opendatahub-operator, sync branch `rhoai`); the architecture-context file pins the exact version and file paths, so the target code is reachable and modifiable. |\n15\t| architecture_claims | 1 | The epic's premise is corroborated by `.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.6-ea.1/rhods-operator.md`: it confirms the go-logr/logr dependency (v1.4.3, runtime-observability), the DataScienceCluster/DSCInitialization CRDs, the component and service controllers (auth, gateway, monitoring), and the action-file/controller architecture. The SME's five-tier count refines (does not contradict) the context file's three-tier summary by counting the module controller and DSCI separately. |\n16\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-817a130d001c7665","agent_id":"a7f75959f8ec75cb2","session_id":"97c222b8-155b-40d2-9c11-f4c45b15208c","uuid":"ad9bbf1a-c781-4c2f-b3e1-fc81fe08c146","timestamp":"2026-10-25T19:39:48.738Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-906ec05f1aa4f933","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Re-review RHAISTRAT-8010"}
